const pageData = JSON.parse("{\"subject\":\"Physics\",\"title\":\"Computation convention\",\"eyebrow\":\"\",\"introduction\":\"\",\"description\":\"\",\"contentHtml\":\"\\u003cp\\u003eHello there, this would be a brief extension of scientific notation, showing some other computation conventions in physics. This section includes \\\"significant figure\\\" convention.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eOne convention is about approximation. Suppose that your measured result using a millimeter-precision meterstick is 17.3228m, and you're asked to diminish the digit used of the result (probably with one fewer digit). Then, we will follow the same round-off rule that we learned in primary school (number less than or equal to 4 is ignored; number larger than or equal to 5 is considered so that the previous digit plus 1). If without special conditional requirement, the default remaining decimal is hundredth (0.01).\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eFor addition and subtraction, if the two numbers possess different digits, how do we deal with the digit of final result? The answer is that we tend to preserve the smallest significant figures for the result (i.e. 1.53+3.274=4.81). Then what about multiplication and division? It's still the same that we're preserving the fewest siginificant figures (i.e. 3.12*0.1=0.3). \\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eBut we haven't yet explained what significant figure is, and this concept is related to measurement as well. \\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eIn brief:\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003col\\u003e\\n\\u003cli\\u003eevery non-zero ending digit can be either included or not included in significant digit;\\u003c/li\\u003e\\n\\u003cli\\u003ethe first zero starting from left is not included in significant figure;\\u003c/li\\u003e\\n\\u003cli\\u003ethe remaining ones are included in significant figures unless explicitly being mentioned.\\u003c/li\\u003e\\n\\u003c/ol\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eAnd since we're going to do computations with real data for multiple times later on, another convention is associated with the scientific notation: your final computed result should be in the form of a*10^b (in which a must be larger than or equal to 1).\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eUp til now, these are the conventions that we do care about in physics, and a reminder is that you do not need to follow them as some rigorous standard. Physics's rigority is not embodied simply by these conventions; some arbitrary conventions are indeed annoying and inconvenient. We'll know them soon.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\",\"children\":[],\"parentTitle\":\"Basis of Measurement \\u0026 Units\",\"parentUrl\":\"/physics/interesting-topics/classical-mechanics/basis-of-measurement-and-units/\"}");
